  "live_records": {
    "q-10844": {
      "dom_id": "step-q10844",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=2{x}^{2}-5x$ at the point $(3,3).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{2}-5x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}-5x)=4x-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(3)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3)=4(3)-5=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=7$ and the coordinates of our point $(3,3),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(3) & =7(x-3) \\\\ y & =7x-21+3 \\\\ y & =7x-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
