    "q-103326": {
      "dom_id": "step-q103326",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find where the tangent to the curve $y={x}^{4}-5$ at the point $(-1,-4)$ intersects the $x$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-5.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}-5)=4{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(-1)=4(-1)^{3}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-4) & =-4(x+1) \\\\ y & =-4x-4-4 \\\\ y & =-4x-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $x$-axis, we set $y=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-4x-8 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(-2,0).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
