    "q-304068": {
      "dom_id": "step-q304068",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\frac{3x+2}{{x}^{2}}$ at the point $(-1,-1).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=\\frac{3x+2}{{x}^{2}}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3x+2}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{-1})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{-2}-4{x}^{-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(-1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-\\frac{3}{(-1)^{2}}-\\frac{4}{(-1)^{3}} \\\\  & =-3+4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=1$ and the coordinates of our point $(-1,-1),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-1) & =1\\cdot (x-(-1)) \\\\ y+1 & =x+1 \\\\ y & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
